12
00:01:03.487 --> 00:01:06.207
♪ So let Git track your versions. First, the working tree: ♪

13
00:01:06.207 --> 00:01:08.627
♪ the files in your editor — what you type is what you see. ♪

14
00:01:08.627 --> 00:01:11.347
♪ Next, the staging area: your next commit, lined up for the shot. ♪

15
00:01:11.347 --> 00:01:13.667
♪ It starts out as a copy of the last commit you've got; ♪

16
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:16.227
♪ git add copies in the new version of each file you choose — ♪

17
00:01:16.227 --> 00:01:18.677
♪ the rest stay as they were. That's what the next commit will use. ♪

18
00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:21.327
♪ git commit takes the picture of the staging area, just so, ♪

19
00:01:21.327 --> 00:01:23.687
